"build:notebooks":{"site":"build","slug":"notebooks","title":"Notebooks and the kernel","url":"https://build.amaete.com/notebooks/","blurb":"Run cells one by one and watch the kernel remember every variable, with answers and charts right beside the code.","checks":[{"q":"What is the kernel?","a":["The text at the top of the notebook","The Python process that runs cells and keeps the variables","A file that stores your charts"],"ok":1,"why":"The kernel is one running Python process. Every cell you run talks to it, and it remembers every variable."},{"q":"You changed cell 1 and reran it and the chart, but not the cells between. What is the danger?","a":["None, the chart is enough","The other outputs still describe the old data: hidden state","The notebook file gets deleted"],"ok":1,"why":"The kernel still holds values from the old run, so the page shows a mix of old and new answers."},{"q":"How do you check a notebook is honest?","a":["Restart the kernel and run all cells from the top","Save it twice","Run the last cell again"],"ok":0,"why":"A fresh kernel knows nothing, so running top to bottom proves the page alone produces the answers."}]},
"build:python-for-ai":{"site":"build","slug":"python-for-ai","title":"Python for AI: lists, arrays and traps","url":"https://build.amaete.com/python-for-ai/","blurb":"See why a Python list of numbers is a row of pointers and a NumPy array is one solid block, and race them on a sum.","checks":[{"q":"Why is a Python loop over a million numbers slow?","a":["Python can't add","Each number is a separate object reached through a pointer and checked one at a time","The numbers are too big"],"ok":1,"why":"A list holds pointers to objects. NumPy keeps raw numbers in one block and loops in compiled code."},{"q":"grid = [[0]*3]*3, then grid[0][0] = 5. What happens?","a":["Only row 0 changes","All three rows show 5","An error"],"ok":1,"why":"The three rows are the same list. Use [[0]*3 for _ in range(3)]."},{"q":"You loop over a generator of batches for two epochs. What does epoch 2 see?","a":["The same batches again","Nothing: the generator is used up","The batches in reverse"],"ok":1,"why":"A generator yields each item once. Make a new one each epoch, or keep a list."}]},
"build:terminal-pipes":{"site":"build","slug":"terminal-pipes","title":"The terminal: pipes and sessions","url":"https://build.amaete.com/terminal-pipes/","blurb":"Pour a training log through a pipeline of small commands until one number comes out: the best loss.","checks":[{"q":"What does the pipe | do?","a":["Sends one command's output into the next command","Deletes a file","Runs a command on a server"],"ok":0,"why":"Each tool does one job; the pipe chains them so the output of one is the input of the next."},{"q":"You trained over ssh without tmux and closed your laptop. What happened?","a":["Training sped up","Training was killed when the connection dropped","Nothing"],"ok":1,"why":"A job tied to the ssh window dies with it. tmux keeps the session alive on the server."},{"q":"What is the difference between > and >>?","a":["None","> replaces the file; >> adds to the end",">> is faster"],"ok":1,"why":"A single arrow empties the file first. Two arrows append."}]},
"build:gpu-basics":{"site":"build","slug":"gpu-basics","title":"GPUs: why AI needs graphics cards","url":"https://build.amaete.com/gpu-basics/","blurb":"Fill the same grid of sums with a CPU's eight strong cores and a graphics card's hundreds of small ones.","checks":[{"q":"Why is a graphics card fast at AI work?","a":["Its cores are each faster than a CPU core","It has thousands of small cores that work on many cells at once","It has a bigger hard drive"],"ok":1,"why":"Matrix multiplication splits into many independent sums, and a GPU does them all at the same time."},{"q":"About how much memory does a 7 billion parameter model need at half precision (fp16)?","a":["About 14 GB, plus some room to work","About 7 MB","About 700 GB"],"ok":0,"why":"Two bytes per parameter: 7 billion × 2 bytes is 14 GB, and the working numbers need a bit more."},{"q":"An 8 × 8 multiply runs slower on the GPU than the CPU. Why?","a":["The GPU is broken","Copying the numbers to the card takes longer than the tiny sum","GPUs cannot multiply small numbers"],"ok":1,"why":"Every job pays for the trip to the card and back. Only big jobs make the trip worth it."}]},
